
SQL Server Indexes - Internals


1) Clustered Index (B-tree) Traversal
	• A clustered index is the physical organization of the table.
	• The leaf level of the clustered index is the actual data rows.
	• Higher levels (root, intermediate) store index keys and pointers (file_id:page_id:slot_id) to lower levels.
👉 How SQL Server finds a value (example: find CustomerID = 42):

[Root Page]
   - Contains upper bound keys + pointers to Intermediate pages
   |
   v
[Intermediate Page(s)]
   - Narrow down search range (e.g., 1–50, 51–100, …)
   |
   v
[Leaf Page(s)]
   - Contains actual data rows (with clustering key)

Clustered Index Traversal:

[Root Page: File 1, Page 100]
   Keys: [50] 
   Pointers: ( <50 → Page 200 , >=50 → Page 300 )
       |
       v
[Intermediate Page: File 1, Page 200]
   Keys: [20, 35] 
   Pointers: (<20 → Page 400 , 20–35 → Page 410, >35 → Page 420)
       |
       v
[Leaf Page: File 1, Page 400]
   Data rows with clustering keys 1–20
   Example Row:
      Slot 0: CustomerID=15, Name="Alice"
      Slot 1: CustomerID=18, Name="Bob"



2) Nonclustered Index (B-tree) Traversal
	• A nonclustered index has its own B-tree structure.
	• The leaf level does not contain full data rows, but contains:
		○ Key columns of the nonclustered index
		○ Plus either:
			§ The clustering key (if the table has a clustered index)
			§ The RID (File:Page:Slot) if the table is a heap
👉 Traversal is the same (root → intermediate → leaf), but after reaching the leaf:
	• SQL Server uses the bookmark lookup (clustering key or RID) to jump to the actual row in the clustered index (or heap).
	
Non Clustered Index Traversal

[Root Page: File 1, Page 500]
   Keys: [M]
   Pointers: (<M → Page 600 , >=M → Page 700)
       |
       v
[Leaf Page: File 1, Page 600]
   Entries:
      Slot 0: LastName="Adams", ClusteringKey=15
      Slot 1: LastName="Allen", ClusteringKey=18
   |
   +--> Use ClusteringKey (e.g., 18) to jump into Clustered Index leaf
        and fetch the actual row


Structural Navigation in SQL Server Index:

-- Enable trace flag to allow DBCC PAGE output
DBCC TRACEON(3604);

-- Check index structure
DBCC IND('TestDB', 'Customers', 1);   -- 1 = clustered index (usually PK)

-- Suppose it shows:
-- PageFID  PagePID  IAMFID  IAMPNO  ObjectID  IndexID  PartitionNumber  PageType
-- 1        100      ...     ...     ...       1        1                2 (Index page - root)
-- 1        200      ...     ...     ...       1        1                2 (Index page - intermediate)
-- 1        400      ...     ...     ...       1        1                1 (Data page - leaf)

-- Look inside a page (Root)
DBCC PAGE('TestDB', 1, 100, 3);

-- Sample Output (simplified):
PAGE HEADER:
  m_pageId = (1:100)
  m_level = 2 (Root)
  m_type = 2 (Index page)

DATA:
  Slot 0, key = 50 → child page = (1:200)
  Slot 1, key = 100 → child page = (1:300)

-- Look inside a leaf page
DBCC PAGE('TestDB', 1, 400, 3);

-- Sample Output (simplified):
PAGE HEADER:
  m_pageId = (1:400)
  m_level = 0 (Leaf)
  m_type = 1 (Data page)

DATA:
  Slot 0, Key = 15, Name="Alice"
  Slot 1, Key = 18, Name="Bob"

1. Logical vs Physical Order
📘 Logical Order
	• Determined by the index key values.
	• Example: In a clustered index on CustomerID, rows should be logically ordered: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 ….
	• SQL Server maintains this order in the B-tree structure.
📀 Physical Order
	• The actual placement of pages on disk (FileID:PageID).
	• Ideally, as you traverse the index, the next logical key’s row should be on the next physical page.
When Fragmentation Happens
	• If a page split occurs (due to inserts/updates), SQL Server may put the new page somewhere else in the file instead of next to the current page.
	• Then, the next-page pointer (in the page header) points to a page that’s far away.
This mismatch is external fragmentation.

Arrow & Dash Diagram of Fragmentation
Logical order:  1 ----> 2 ----> 3 ----> 4
Physical order: Page(100) -> Page(101) -> Page(102) -> Page(103)

Fragmented:
Logical order:  1 ----> 2 ----> 3 ----> 4
Physical order: Page(100) -> Page(450) -> Page(900) -> Page(200)

DBCC PAGE Illustration

DBCC TRACEON(3604);

-- Show index page details
DBCC IND('TestDB', 'Customers', 1);
PageFID  PagePID  PageType  IndexLevel
1        100      1 (Data)  0
1        101      1 (Data)  0
1        200      1 (Data)  0
1        450      1 (Data)  0

DBCC PAGE('TestDB', 1, 100, 3);

PAGE HEADER:
  m_pageId = (1:100)
  m_level = 0
  m_type = 1 (Data)
  m_nextPage = (1:450)
  m_prevPage = (NULL)

DATA:
  Slot 0: Key = 1
  Slot 1: Key = 2

DBCC PAGE('TestDB', 1, 450, 3);

PAGE HEADER:
  m_pageId = (1:450)
  m_prevPage = (1:100)
  m_nextPage = (1:200)

DATA:
  Slot 0: Key = 3
  Slot 1: Key = 4

• Logical order: 1,2 → 3,4 (correct, by index key).
• Physical order: Page 100 → Page 450 → Page 200 (out of order).
This is exactly external fragmentation: logical adjacency ≠ physical adjacency.

Types of Fragmentation
External Fragmentation (Extent/Page level)
	• Definition: Logical order of pages (by key) ≠ Physical order on disk (PageIDs).
	• Cause: Page splits, frequent inserts/updates.
	• Effect: SQL Server can’t use sequential read-ahead; more random IO.

Internal Fragmentation (Inside a page)
	• Definition: Page has lots of free space / unused slots.
	• Cause: Deletes, updates that shrink rows, fill factor settings.
Effect: More pages needed to store the same data (wasteful memory/IO).
